                          "String": "ATMOSPHERIC FATE: According to a model of gas/particle partitioning of semivolatile organic compounds in the atmosphere(1), glutamic acid, which has an extrapolated vapor pressure of 1.7X10-8 mm Hg at 25 °C(2), will exist in both the vapor and particulate phases in the ambient atmosphere. Vapor-phase glutamic acid is degraded in the atmosphere by reaction with photochemically-produced hydroxyl radicals(SRC); the half-life for this reaction in air is estimated to be 9.4 hours(SRC), calculated from its rate constant of 4.1X10-11 cu cm/molecule-sec at 25 °C(SRC) that was derived using a structure estimation method(3). Particulate-phase glutamic acid may be removed from the air by wet and dry deposition(SRC). Aliphatic amino acids exhibit no absorption in the UV region above 220 nm(4), therefore glutamic acid is not expected to be susceptible to direct photolysis by sunlight(SRC).",
